I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the provided tungstate guidance first, then use the local indexing/database and GSAS-II tooling to identify and refine all defensible crystalline phases. I’ll preserve the final refinement project and verify every submitted artifact against it.

The chemistry is MnO + WO₃ at 900 °C, so MnWO₄ is an obvious hypothesis, but I’m treating it only as a candidate: the high raw background and unknown specimen/profile effects can conceal residual oxide or secondary manganese tungstates. I’m now mapping the local reference-search and GSAS-II interfaces and extracting the observed peak inventory before committing to phases.

The first library-wide screen is still running. Early parsing warnings are confined to some defective oxide references; they do not affect the primary Mn–W–O candidates, and I’ll reject any structurally invalid source rather than letting it enter refinement.

The observed peak set is already strongly diagnostic: the MnWO₄ reference gives a single-phase correlation of 0.882, more than twice any oxide candidate, and accounts for the dominant reflections from 15.7° through 98°. I’m moving to whole-pattern refinement now; the key test is whether a one-phase wolframite model leaves coherent Bragg residuals that justify a second phase.

A one-phase MnWO₄ Rietveld model has reached Rwp ≈ 6.4% over all 8,301 points and refines to the ambient wolframite cell (about 4.8303 × 5.7561 × 4.9956 Å, β ≈ 91.219°). That is strong phase evidence, but it is not yet at the Poisson limit. I’ve also caught two model-quality issues—an exact duplicate scale parameter and an unconstrained negative roughness term—so those will be removed before judging residual peaks or fractions.

